claude <claude@p7x.dev> writes:

> lab-process-mail answers the inbox only when someone runs it. This
> series adds lab-process-maild, which keeps it running in the
> background under daemon(8):
>
>     lab-process-maild start
>     lab-process-maild status
>     lab-process-maild stop
>
> It ships in the lab-process-mail package, with a man page,
> lab-process-maild(8). The package installs under a LOCALBASE, ~/local
> after "just install", and the daemon is configured the way an rc(8)
> service is, from an rc.conf(5)-style file:
>
>     ${LOCALBASE}/etc/lab-process-maild.conf
>
> The file is sh and sets variables without exporting them. These are
> the settings, with their defaults for a user other than root:
>
>     lab_process_maild_env=""
>     lab_process_maild_interval=60
>     lab_process_maild_logfile=~/log/lab-process-maild.log
>     lab_process_maild_pidfile=~/var/run/lab-process-maild.pid
>
> For root, the log and pidfile default to /var/log and /var/run, so
> neither case needs a conf. start creates the directories of the log
> and pidfile if they are missing. The daemon starts from an empty
> environment, keeping only HOME, USER, LOGNAME, and a PATH that begins
> with ${LOCALBASE}/bin. lab_process_maild_env adds to it, as
> ${name}_env does in rc.subr(8):
>
>     lab_process_maild_env="LAB_BASE_BRANCH=b/dev@upstream \
>         NOTIFY_URL=https://ntfy.sh/my-topic"
>
> stop lets the pass in progress finish before the daemon exits. A
> failed pass still stops the daemon; that behavior and how to fix it
> are described in "[BUG] lab-process-mail gets stuck" [1].

This whole interface is quite nice and basically exactly what I want.

[...]

> -	    PATH="${root}/bin:/sbin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin" \
> +	    PATH="${localbase}/bin:/sbin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin" \

This shouldn't be baked in... if we need to update the PATH, it should
come via env var provided by the config file.

> +		with_env lab-process-mail &

Maybe I'm misunderstanding but it shouldn't need to background the
process, should it? That's exactly what daemon(8) does.

---

This is close but something is off, and I think I see what the problem
is. It's conflating service management with daemonization.

The backgrounding gives it away. The daemon shouldn't need to put the
process in the background - that's what daemon(8) is for.

Maybe it's not so far off, and it's just being lazy and keeping some of
the existing code structure, e.g. the wait, as though that was important
for the design of this program.

In any case, the point of introducing daemon(8) is so that the program
itself can be a one-shot script, but can still be controlled as a
daemon. Backgrounding a process misses the point.
